{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>From twenty years old.</b> Beginning from when he enters the counting." "<b>Sixty years old.</b> An elder, and many hold that this is a decree of the King, for from one month old until five years he gives five shekels — if he passes one day beyond the month he gives five shekels. According to the opinion of the Gaon [Saadia], he gives one shekel per year until five full years, for many say they are four, in the parts of the years, and afterward nineteen full ones, and afterward fifty-nine, to return to the place from which he began, and fifteen to complete the seventy-five, corresponding to the four parts. But according to the truth, all these are full years, or even one day in a year counts as a year. And we rely only on the words of tradition, for what need is there to divide from one month until five, and from five until twenty, if he gives one shekel per year? And one may answer regarding the matter of the month: one who is less than a month old has no valuation. And after sixty he goes back. And further, what does he do after sixty years — if he gives thus always? And all these are errors. And the principle is that it is a decree of Scripture, for if it were on the method of the male's excess over the female, then after sixty he would add a third, and from five until twenty he would add half, and from one month until five and from twenty until sixty he would add a tenth of the half."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:58.650111939Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}